<section class="reference-section" id="tripura-destruction"><div class="reference-section-heading"><h2>Shiva as Tripurantaka · The Destruction of Tripura</h2><a href="/tripura-destruction.html">Return to story</a></div><div class="reference-content"><p><strong>Shiva Mahapurana · Rudra Samhita · Yuddha Khanda · chapters 1–12.</strong> This original condensed English retelling follows the owner-supplied Gita Press Sanskrit–Hindi edition. The private PDF is not copied into the public website or repository.</p><p><strong>Supplied edition:</strong> Part I, private PDF pages 833–882; printed pages 823–872. The page range begins with the austerities and boon of Taraka’s three sons and ends with Maya’s refuge and the departure of the devas.</p><details><summary>Chapter sequence behind the reader<span aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><ol><li><strong>Chapter 1</strong><small>Tarakaksha, Kamalaksha and Vidyunmali; Brahma’s boon; the gold, silver and iron cities built by Maya.</small></li><li><strong>Chapters 2–3</strong><small>The devas appeal to Brahma and Shiva; the inhabitants’ merit and devotion initially make violent destruction improper; Vishnu considers another means.</small></li><li><strong>Chapters 4–5</strong><small>The extended delusion episode and the decline of Tripura’s former discipline. The public reader condenses the chapter’s polemical religious descriptions.</small></li><li><strong>Chapters 6–7</strong><small>The devas praise Shiva, undertake the prescribed recitation and receive his assurance.</small></li><li><strong>Chapter 8</strong><small>The construction of the cosmic chariot, bow and arrow from divine and cosmic powers.</small></li><li><strong>Chapter 9</strong><small>Brahma as charioteer, Shiva’s departure and the Pashupati context.</small></li><li><strong>Chapter 10</strong><small>Ganesha’s worship, the rare alignment of the cities and Shiva’s single arrow burning Tripura.</small></li><li><strong>Chapters 11–12</strong><small>The devas’ praise, Shiva’s blessing, Maya’s refuge and his protected dwelling in Vitala.</small></li></ol></details><p><strong>Interpretive boundary.</strong> The three cities, three metals, rare alignment and cosmic chariot belong to the supplied narrative. Later inward readings of Tripura as ego, impurity, bondage, three bodies or other triads vary across Shaiva teaching traditions; the reader labels these as interpretation rather than translating them into the source.</p><p>All three images are newly AI-generated devotional interpretations. Architecture, cosmic mechanics, clothing, armies and landscape are imagined. The images do not claim to reconstruct a historical city or reproduce every item in the Purana’s cosmic catalogue.</p></div></section>
